WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.536
What fine-tuning is good at.

2
00:00:01.816 --> 00:00:05.421
Providers’ own documentation converges on a short list.

3
00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:18.992
OpenAI’s model optimization guide recommends supervised fine-tuning for classification, nuanced translation, producing content in a specific format, and correcting failures to follow instructions.

4
00:00:19.272 --> 00:00:34.824
Google’s supervised tuning page for Gemini lists classification, summarization, extractive question answering, and chat, and suggests tuning when prompts don’t produce consistent results, when a task is too complex to describe in a prompt,

5
00:00:35.104 --> 00:00:39.051
or when you want to drop long few-shot examples from every request.

6
00:00:39.331 --> 00:00:43.064
Group those into four things fine-tuning changes: Behavior.

7
00:00:43.344 --> 00:00:47.781
When to answer, when to abstain, when to escalate, which fields to leave null.

8
00:00:48.061 --> 00:00:48.829
Format.

9
00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:53.056
Always this JSON shape, this house template, this query language.

10
00:00:53.336 --> 00:00:54.125
Style.

11
00:00:54.405 --> 00:00:57.904
Tone, length, vocabulary, the voice of your support team.

12
00:00:58.184 --> 00:00:59.699
Narrow-task accuracy.

13
00:00:59.979 --> 00:01:07.083
A small model trained on thousands of examples of one task can match or beat a large general model on that task.

14
00:01:07.363 --> 00:01:09.325
The research is consistent with this.

15
00:01:09.605 --> 00:01:28.699
LIMA (Zhou and colleagues, 2023) fine-tuned a 65-billion-parameter model on only 1,000 carefully curated examples and in a human study its responses were rated equivalent to or better than GPT-4’s in 43 percent of comparisons;

16
00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:35.784
the authors argue that almost all knowledge is learned in pretraining and a small amount of tuning teaches the output style.

17
00:01:36.064 --> 00:01:51.872
LoRA Land (Zhao and colleagues, 2024) fine-tuned 310 models across 10 base models and 31 narrow tasks and reports that the fine-tuned models beat GPT-4 by 10 points on average on those tasks.

18
00:01:52.152 --> 00:02:06.573
That second result comes from a vendor’s technical report and compares against a 2024 model, so read it as evidence that narrow fine-tuning can beat a general model on a narrow task, not as a ranking of today’s models.

19
00:02:06.853 --> 00:02:10.672
Notice what is not on the list: teaching the model facts it doesn’t know.
